{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cf68T9UR6xAJfQDtc4gLd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CZ2Pc1CmgVMYTPSkxn2Qm5","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"1000genome dftracer pipeline STEP 1-7","subagent_type":"claude","run_in_background":true,"prompt":"You are running the dftracer-pipeline skill autonomously for the 1000genome-workflow app, SCOPED TO STEPS 1 THROUGH 7 ONLY (stop after STEP 7 — do NOT do optimization/lessons-sync/final report steps 8-10).\n\nBefore anything else, load these skills with mcp__dftracer__skill_load: \"dftracer-pipeline\" (full), \"dftracer-mcp-first\", \"dftracer-annotation-lessons\" (SKILL.md) then skill_load(name=\"dftracer-annotation-lessons\", file=\"LESSONS_LOG.md\"), \"workload-1000genome\", \"dftracer-annotate-python\", \"dftracer-system-detect\". Apply every lesson relevant to Python annotation and to 1000genome.\n\nINPUTS:\n- APP_URL = https://github.com/pegasus-isi/1000genome-workflow\n- REF = master\n- SMOKE_CMD = python3 bin/individuals.py ALL.chr1.tiny.vcf 1 0 300 300\n- This is a PURE PYTHON workflow — nothing to compile. Use the Python annotation MCP path (python_annotate_project / python_annotate_file etc, or dispatch to the dftracer-annotate-python subagent / dftracer-annotator), NEVER the clang/C path.\n- Do NOT install or use Pegasus, Condor, or PMC — bin/ task scripts run standalone; that's all this smoke test needs.\n- ANNOTATION SCOPE: annotate bin/individuals.py ONLY — it is ~82% of workflow wall time. Its VCF read loop and its per-individual file-write + tar-compress path are where the I/O is — make sure both get instrumented, not just a single span per function.\n- DFTRACER VERSION CHECK: after mcp__dftracer__session_install_dftracer, run `pip show dftracer` in the session venv and report the version. It MUST be a real release version (e.g. 3.x.y) — a 0.0.x version means the clone lost its git tags. If you see 0.0.x, fix (re-clone with tags / reinstall) rather than continuing.\n- INPUT DATA: staged READ-ONLY at /p/lustre3/haridev/1kg-eval/data, containing ALL.chr1.tiny.vcf (300 VCF data rows) and columns.txt. individuals.py reads these from ITS WORKING DIRECTORY. Copy (don't symlink, don't modify the staged originals) those two files into the run directory before running the smoke test / trace run.\n- SUCCESS CRITERION FOR STEP 7: a .pfw or .pfw.gz trace file must exist after the dftracer run, and you must report its exact path, file size, and EVENT COUNT (use dftracer MCP trace-reading tools — mcp__dftracer__event_count / mcp__dftracer__reader / mcp__dftracer__stats — never hand-rolled gzip/json parsing). The event count must reflect the per-individual write path (many events), not just a handful (one span per function call is a FAILURE of this run — it means the write/tar-compress loop was not actually instrumented or not exercised). If the trace is thin, diagnose and re-annotate/re-run before declaring STEP 7 done.\n\nTOOL POLICY (mandatory, no exceptions):\n- If an MCP tool exists for a job, it is the ONLY way to do that job. Never hand-roll with Bash what a tool does, even if the tool just failed and you can see the shell command that would work.\n- A failing MCP tool is a REPAIR TASK: read the error, fix the tool code under src/dftracer_agents/mcp_tools/tools/ (the server runs with --reload over HTTP, so an edit goes live within a second or two — just re-call the tool after editing, no restart needed, no need to ask anyone), then re-call it and continue through it.\n- Record every repair: a lesson on the relevant skill (mcp-first or workload-1000genome), and if it's a fact about the workload rather than the tool, also consider a memory note (though per project policy, personal/session-identifying details are never persisted — anonymize any memory write: no absolute user paths, no job ids, no session UUIDs).\n- If a tool cannot be repaired within a reasonable budget (a couple of focused attempts), STOP that step and report the defect honestly rather than working around it and claiming success.\n- Bash is fine for: inspecting the tree, reading logs, verifying a fix worked, `pip show dftracer`, file copies of the two input files, `ls -lh` on trace output.\n- Never use sudo. Never build/run/smoke-test from the project root — always cwd into workspaces/<session>/... (this is a NEW session workspace you create fresh below; do not reuse or resume any existing 1000genome session under workspaces/, even if one already exists — it was built on a different machine).\n\nTHIS IS A NEW RUN ON A DIFFERENT MACHINE. Call mcp__dftracer__session_create yourself to open a brand-new session against the CURRENT system (confirm via mcp__dftracer__system_detect first). Do not resume any prior session.\n\nSTEP TRACKING: You do not have a TodoWrite tool in this environment, so instead: before starting each of the 7 steps, state in one sentence \"STEP N: <name> — starting\" and after it completes \"STEP N: <name> — done (<one-line result>)\". If a step fails and you retry it, say so explicitly (\"STEP N retry 1: <reason>\"). Call mcp__dftracer__profile_bind once right after session_create succeeds (this is when profiling starts, per project policy), then bracket every step's work with mcp__dftracer__profile_step_begin(step=\"STEP N: <name>\") / mcp__dftracer__profile_step_end with the exact same step string, so retries show up as retries not new steps.\n\nTHE SEVEN STEPS (per the dftracer-pipeline skill, condensed for Python/no-compile):\n\nSTEP 1 (already answered by the args above — just print \"Starting pipeline for 1000genome-workflow @ master\" and move on).\n\nSTEP 2 — SESSION SETUP:\n  a. session_create(url=APP_URL, ref=REF) → get RUN_ID, WS. Get canonical paths via session_get_run_paths — never hand-build paths like \"ws/build_ann\".\n  b. mcp__dftracer__profile_bind right after session_create succeeds.\n  c. This is a pure Python app with no compiled build step — session_detect should report build_tool=python (or none/script). Do NOT run cmake/configure steps. If session_configure/session_build_install are required by the tool flow for a python project, call them; otherwise skip straight to installing dftracer.\n  d. session_install_dftracer(run_id=RUN_ID) — must land dftracer in the SAME venv the app will run in. After it succeeds, `pip show dftracer` in that venv and REPORT the version; if it's 0.0.x, treat as a failure, fix (likely the dftracer clone lost git tags — reinstall from the correct source/tag) and retry.\n  e. session_copy_annotated(run_id=RUN_ID) — copies source into annotated/.\n  f. session_validate_structure(run_id=RUN_ID); if clean=false, session_reorganize_structure(run_id=RUN_ID, dry_run=False) then re-validate.\n\nSTEP 3 — ANNOTATION (Python path only, bin/individuals.py only):\n  Use mcp__dftracer__python_annotate_project or python_annotate_file (or dispatch via the Agent tool to the \"dftracer-annotate-python\" subagent with model=\"sonnet\" — prefer this dispatch since it is specialized) scoped to bin/individuals.py in the annotated/ tree. Make sure the VCF read loop and the per-individual write+tar-compress path both get instrumented (likely multiple functions/call sites, not just the top-level main). Follow the Python annotation rules from the skill you loaded (no @staticmethod log_static, no numba/torch.jit decorators, no __len__ — not applicable here but keep in mind for any similar traps). Use python_extract_functions / python_estimate_function_cost as needed to decide what to instrument if the auto path under-covers the write path.\n  When spawning the dftracer-annotate-python subagent via the Agent tool, use model=\"sonnet\".\n\nSTEP 4 — VALIDATION:\n  Run mcp__dftracer__python_lint_annotations and mcp__dftracer__validate_annotations(run_id=RUN_ID, language=\"python\") on the annotated file(s). Fix any findings by targeted re-annotation (python_annotate_file with overrides), never manual Edit of