me\" or the first component of RUN_ID).\n  e. session_analyze_traces(run_id=RUN_ID, query_type=\"summary\").\n  f. Use mcp__dftracer__event_count / mcp__dftracer__reader / mcp__dftracer__stats (MCP trace tools only) to get the exact trace file path, size, and EVENT COUNT. Confirm the event count is large enough to reflect per-individual write+compress activity (should scale with ~300 individuals' worth of I/O operations, not just a handful of spans). If it looks thin, that is a STEP 7 failure — diagnose (was the write/tar path actually annotated and exercised? did the smoke test really iterate all individuals given the args \"1 0 300 300\"?) and fix before finishing.\n\nAfter STEP 7 completes (or if it genuinely cannot be made to pass after real repair attempts), write a session report. Since this run is scoped to stop after STEP 7, do NOT call session_final_report, session_lessons_sync_pr, or the optimization steps. Instead:\n  - Write a concise Markdown report to <WS>/artifacts/step1_7_report.md covering: RUN_ID, dftracer version confirmed, files/functions annotated (VCF read loop + write/tar path coverage confirmed), build/smoke test result, trace file path+size+event count, any MCP tool repairs made (with what was fixed), and any lessons recorded.\n  - Record any genuinely new, non-obvious lessons to the appropriate skill (workload-1000genome and/or dftracer-mcp-first) using the project's skill-writing conventions — anonymize (no absolute user paths/usernames/job ids; use $HOME, $PROJECT_ROOT, <session> placeholders per project policy).\n\nAt the very end, report back a summary (under 400 words) covering: RUN_ID, dftracer version, annotation coverage summary, trace path/size/event count, pass/fail on the STEP 7 success criterion, and any tool defects you found/fixed along the way. This is fully autonomous — do not stop to ask questions; if genuinely blocked (e.g. a repair is impossible within budget), stop and report the defect clearly rather than fabricating su